  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the structural presence of a sure outcome, not on the psychological weight or evaluation of risk. The reason prefers the lottery without a sure outcome—Lottery B—because only A has a 100% outcome. The individual’s verbal report highlights that the loss in A is guaranteed (sure outcome) and contrasts it with the very low chance of loss in B (no sure negative outcome). This shows that the individual recognized the presence of a sure loss in A and preferred avoiding it, which aligns with the structural feature (presence of sure outcome) emphasized by the decision reason. While the individual frames it in terms of risk-taking and probability of loss, the core of their deliberation hinges on the certainty of the outcome in A versus uncertainty in B—exactly the information the decision reason uses. Although the justification is phrased in behavioral terms (‘willing to take the risk’), the underlying comparison matches the reason’s rule. Therefore, the individual appears to base their decision on the presence or absence of a sure outcome, consistent with the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

